; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   291   \DESCRIPTION    A function is called with one or more pointer parameters, which are pointing to local variables or
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   292                   are passed to the calling function.
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   293   \COUNTERMEASURE \N No measure is required, because local variables are always valid.
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   294  
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   295  \ID SBSW_OS_HAL_PWA_FIRST_PCXI
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   296   \DESCRIPTION    A write access is performed on a pointer, which points to the memory address of the CSA frame. The
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   297                   memory address is derived from "Context->InitPCXI". The validity of "Context" is ensured by the
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   298                   caller. The content of "Context->InitPCXI" is also valid, because it is always initialized, before
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   299                   this pointer write access is performed. If "Context" is a task context, then its "InitPCXI" is
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   300                   initialized in Os_Hal_ContextInit and is re-initialized in Os_Hal_ContextReset and
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   301                   Os_Hal_ContextResetAndResume. If "Context" is an ISR/Hook context, then its "InitPCXI" is either
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   302                   initialized in Os_Hal_ContextInit or re-initialized in Os_Hal_Abort, when the ISR is killed.
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   303                   As a result, whenever Os_Hal_ContextIntCleanupCSAs is called, "Context->InitPCXI" always contains
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   304                   a valid value. Thus, this write access is always performed on a valid pointer.
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   305   \COUNTERMEASURE \N No measure is required, because its validity is ensured by code review.
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   306  
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   307  \ID SBSW_OS_HAL_PWA_PCXI
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   308   \DESCRIPTION    A write access is performed on a pointer, which points to the memory address of the CSA frame. The
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   309                   memory address is derived from the PCXI register. Since Os_Hal_Svlcx is previously performed, the
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   310                   PCXI register always contains a valid value. Thus, this write access is always performed on a valid
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   311                   pointer.
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   312   \COUNTERMEASURE \N No measure is required, because its validity is ensured by code review.
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   313  
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   314  \ID SBSW_OS_HAL_PWA_PCXI_INTERRUPT
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   315   \DESCRIPTION    A write access is performed on a pointer, which points to the memory address of the CSA frame. The
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   316                   memory address is derived from the PCXI register. This function is called within the trap handler,
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   317                   where the PCXI register always contains a valid value. Thus, this write access is always performed
; ..\BSW\Os\Os_Hal_Core.c	   318                   on a valid pointer.
